freedreno/ir3: give a shader the branchstack the register can actually hold.

`0009` made the truncation audible. This stops it happening.

`max_branchstack` is 64. The `BRANCHSTACK` field it feeds is **7 bits** on a6xx
and later -- `a6xx.xml`, `low="13" high="19"`, commented "seems to be nesting
level for flow control" -- so the hardware can be told **127**. The cap is half
of what fits, it is not imposed by the register, and it is applied by the
`MIN2` in `ir3_shader_branchstack_hw()` with no error and no fallback.

What that silence costs is in `0009` and is not repeated here, except for the
part that decides this patch: past the cap a diverged thread has nowhere to be
parked, so it is never reactivated, and the wave continues with an active mask
that no longer describes which threads are live. `ballot`, `elect` and the
ballot scans do not merely run under that mask, they are questions *about* it,
so they return wrong answers rather than running slowly.

**Raising a cap is free for every shader that was already under it.** A shader
whose branchstack is 40 does not care where the ceiling sits. The only shaders
this reaches are the ones being silently miscompiled today, which is the whole
argument for the change: it cannot regress a shader that was correct.

**Except in one way, and that is what the guard is for.**
`ir3_get_reg_independent_max_waves()` computes occupancy as
`branchstack_size / branchstack`, and ends with

    if (v->has_barrier && (max_waves < waves_per_wg)) {
       mesa_loge("Compute shader (%s) which has workgroup barrier cannot be
                  used because it's impossible to have enough concurrent
                  waves.", v->name);
       exit(1);
    }

A larger branchstack means fewer concurrent waves, so a larger cap can reach
that `exit(1)` for a compute shader with a barrier and a big workgroup **that
runs today**. Turning a title that renders into a title that dies is not an
improvement, and this repo has shipped that mistake once already -- `07493a0`,
where ARM64X had to become opt-in after regressing a game that worked.

So the cap is now the smaller of the register's 127 and whatever still leaves a
barrier shader its `waves_per_wg` waves. A shader that cannot afford the extra
slots simply does not get them and is exactly as it was before this patch. The
guard is computed for the single threadsize, which gives the larger
`waves_per_wg` and therefore the smaller cap -- the conservative direction, and
the only one that cannot introduce the failure it exists to prevent.

Scoped to `has_dual_wave_dispatch`, which is a8xx alone. That is the generation
whose second reconvergence pass counts every two-way branch as divergent and so
reaches high branchstacks in the first place, and the only one with no list
under `src/freedreno/ci`. Nothing changes for a6xx or a7xx, which pass the
subgroup and reconvergence suites today.

This is still not the fix for a shader that needs more than 127. Spilling the
branchstack remains the answer to that and remains an open TODO upstream; `0009`
is what says so out loud when it happens.

diff --git a/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_compiler.c b/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_compiler.c
index f0201e9170e..0ef77ee4979 100644
--- a/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_compiler.c
+++ b/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_compiler.c
@@ -273,7 +273,19 @@ ir3_compiler_create(struct fd_device *dev, const struct fd_dev_id *dev_id,
 
    /* TODO see if older GPU's were different here */
    compiler->branchstack_size = dev_info->props.has_dual_wave_dispatch ? 512 : 256;
-   compiler->max_branchstack = 64;
+   /* The BRANCHSTACK field is 7 bits wide on a6xx and later
+    * (`a6xx.xml`, low=13 high=19, "nesting level for flow control"), so 127
+    * is what the hardware can be told. 64 is half of that, and the cap is not
+    * a limit the register imposes -- it is applied by `MIN2` in
+    * `ir3_shader_branchstack_hw()`, which truncates in silence. Past the cap
+    * a diverged thread has nowhere to be parked, is never reactivated, and
+    * the wave runs on with an active mask that no longer says who is live;
+    * ballot, elect and the ballot scans then answer questions about that mask
+    * and answer them wrongly. Raising it costs occupancy for the shaders that
+    * need it and nothing at all for the ones that do not, because a shader
+    * under the old cap is unaffected by where the new one sits.
+    */
+   compiler->max_branchstack = compiler->gen >= 6 ? 127 : 64;
 
    compiler->max_variable_workgroup_size = 1024;
 
diff --git a/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_shader.h b/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_shader.h
index c9eb4b2822d..2d64847d6b9 100644
--- a/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_shader.h
+++ b/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_shader.h
@@ -1486,7 +1486,41 @@ ir3_shader_branchstack_hw(const struct ir3_shader_variant *v)
    if (v->compiler->gen < 5)
       return v->branchstack;
 
-   return align(MIN2(v->branchstack, v->compiler->max_branchstack), 2);
+   uint32_t cap = v->compiler->max_branchstack;
+
+   /* A workgroup barrier turns a branchstack that is merely expensive into a
+    * hang, and the driver would rather kill the process than produce one:
+    * `ir3_get_reg_independent_max_waves()` calls `exit(1)` when a shader with
+    * a barrier cannot get `waves_per_wg` waves running at once. Occupancy
+    * there is `branchstack_size / branchstack`, so raising the cap lowers it,
+    * and a bigger cap could newly reach that exit for a title that runs
+    * today. Refusing the extra slots is much better than that: the shader is
+    * then no worse off than it was before the cap moved.
+    *
+    * Computed for the single threadsize, which yields the larger
+    * `waves_per_wg` and so the smaller cap -- the conservative direction, and
+    * the one that cannot introduce the failure it exists to avoid.
+    */
+   if (v->has_barrier && !v->local_size_variable &&
+       v->compiler->info->props.has_dual_wave_dispatch) {
+      const uint32_t threads =
+         v->local_size[0] * v->local_size[1] * v->local_size[2];
+      const uint32_t granularity = v->compiler->info->wave_granularity;
+      const uint32_t per_wave =
+         v->compiler->info->threadsize_base * granularity;
+
+      if (threads && per_wave && granularity) {
+         const uint32_t waves_per_wg =
+            DIV_ROUND_UP(threads, per_wave) * granularity;
+         const uint32_t affordable =
+            v->compiler->branchstack_size / waves_per_wg;
+
+         if (affordable > 0)
+            cap = MIN2(cap, affordable);
+      }
+   }
+
+   return align(MIN2(v->branchstack, cap), 2);
 }
 
 ENDC;
